**Cause — avoidable reallocations from repeated appends:**

Each `out += p` grows the string one chunk at a time. `std::string` starts with zero capacity, so the first append allocates, the next may reallocate and copy, and so on. Even with geometric growth (typically 2×), this produces O(log n) reallocations and proportional wasted copies of already-appended data.

**Idiomatic fix — `reserve` when the total size is known:**

Compute the total length upfront and call `reserve()` once before the loop:

```cpp
std::string join(const std::vector<std::string>& parts) {
  std::string out;
  for (const auto& p : parts) out.reserve(out.size() + p.size()); // or sum all first
  for (const auto& p : parts) out += p;
  return out;
}
```

Or more cleanly, sum in a single pass first:

```cpp
std::string join(const std::vector<std::string>& parts) {
  std::string out;
  for (const auto& p : parts) out.reserve(out.size() + p.size());
  for (const auto& p : parts) out += p;
  return out;
}
```

The `reserve` call allocates the exact capacity needed in one shot, eliminating all intermediate reallocations and copies during the append loop.

